 1.1 For each point $p$, use [F1] to choose a neighborhood $U$ and a smooth future-directed tetrad $E$ there. In a local oriented coordinate frame, the determinant of the component matrix of $E$ is a smooth nonzero real function; shrink $U$ to a connected neighborhood of $p$, so its sign is constant. If that sign is negative, replace $E_1$ by $-E_1$. This preserves orthonormality and the future direction of $E_0$ and makes $E$ positively oriented by [F2]. Thus positively oriented future tetrads exist locally. [given, A1, F1, F2, algebra]
 
-2.1 Let $u,v$ lie in a common fiber of $P^+_g(M)$. Since $u$ is an isomorphism, $A:=u^{-1}v$ is the unique real matrix with $v=u\circ A$. The isometry condition gives $A^{\mathsf T}\eta A=\eta$. Positivity of the two frame orientations gives $\det A>0$, while the Lorentz identity gives $\det A=\pm1$, hence $\det A=1$. Because $u(e_0)$ and $v(e_0)$ are future timelike, $A e_0$ is future timelike; so $A$ preserves the selected future component and belongs to $SO^+(1,3)$. This proves that the right action is transitive on each fiber; uniqueness of $A$ proves freeness. [given, F3, algebra]
